  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "giLzhJAGlA76LzRbnzRTNW7bujQ=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:38.456Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The screen reads CLEVR as right on the two attributes it usually weights most heavily \u2014 size (250+ staff) and lineage \u2014 and wrong on the one that overrides them: the incumbent. Its published stack is Mendix and Siemens, which means the app layer a Powerhouse engagement would sit in is already occupied by a proprietary platform the firm sells for a living. That is what puts a well-matched Dutch integrator at priority 9, do not pitch.\n\nThe mechanism is commercial rather than technical. A partner's platform relationship is not a neutral technology preference; it is the basis on which the delivery business is priced and staffed. A firm earning against Mendix licences and Mendix-certified developers has no reason to carry a typed-spec alternative into the same account, because the alternative competes with the thing that pays for the bench.\n\nWhat the assessment does add is a ranking inside the excluded set: CLEVR is the best of the Mendix cohort if conversion is ever pursued. That is a different, slower motion from partnership \u2014 it asks the firm to trade a platform relationship rather than add a layer beside it. Read practically, CLEVR is the first name to call if a conversion programme gets funded, and nothing to do before then.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- CLEVR (NL \u00b7 250+)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Right size and right lineage, wrong incumbent. Best of the Mendix cohort if you pursue conversion.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:25:38.456780Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:38.456785Z"
    }
  },
